WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.363
In comm mutants, Slit is still at the midline, and Robo is still made.

2
00:00:08.263 --> 00:00:09.663
Yet almost no axon crosses.

3
00:00:10.683 --> 00:00:11.683
Why?

4
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:13.607
The rule offers one answer.

5
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:15.947
The axon hears Slit too early.

6
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:19.807
If so, Comm's job is to keep Robo down before crossing.

7
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.980
How would you test that with genetics alone?

8
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.580
In 1998, Tear, Goodman and colleagues did two experiments.

9
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:31.453
First, make a fly lacking both robo and comm.

10
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:35.013
Will it look like comm, like robo, or something new?

11
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:42.137
It looks like robo.

12
00:00:43.077 --> 00:00:44.257
Follow it step by step.

13
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.240
In a comm mutant, Robo is too high before crossing.

14
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.380
Axons are repelled early and do not cross.

15
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.290
In the double mutant there is no Robo1 at all.

16
00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.850
So there is no Robo1 to be too high.

17
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:01.813
With Robo1 gone, losing Comm changes nothing.

18
00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:04.733
The embryo shows only the robo phenotype.

19
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:07.807
This reasoning is called epistasis.

20
00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:12.927
When one gene only turns another down, the double looks like the gene turned down.

21
00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:14.987
That is the downstream gene.

22
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.540
Second experiment, the reverse.

23
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.820
Make Comm in every neuron, at a modest level.

24
00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:24.680
What happens to the ladder?

25
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:33.913
Robo protein falls, and the ladder looks like robo.

26
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:38.213
Ipsilateral axons cross, and commissural axons recross.

27
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:42.477
Extra Comm everywhere turns Slit hearing down everywhere.

28
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:46.437
Much stronger Comm can give a more severe, slit-like collapse.

29
00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:49.237
So Comm is not a second cue.

30
00:01:50.097 --> 00:01:52.537
It sets how much Robo the axon has, and when.

31
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.840
How does it do that?

32
00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.880
In 2002, Keleman, Dickson and colleagues proposed a sorting model.

33
00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:07.123
Comm is made in commissural neurons for a short time, before and during crossing.

34
00:02:09.583 --> 00:02:12.323
Inside the neuron, it meets newly made Robo.

35
00:02:13.083 --> 00:02:17.443
It diverts it away from the surface, into late endosomes and lysosomes.

36
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:19.923
There, Robo is destroyed.

37
00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:22.843
The growth cone crosses carrying little Robo.

38
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:29.357
Once the axon has crossed, the neuron stops making Comm.

39
00:02:30.097 --> 00:02:31.517
Robo reaches the growth cone.

40
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.440
Now the Slit it just crossed repels it.

41
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.500
Sorting is real. In living embryos, Comm stops a tagged Robo from reaching the axon

42
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.500
tip.

43
00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:51.373
But it is not the whole story.

44
00:02:52.353 --> 00:02:55.053
Flies whose Robo cannot be sorted develop normally.

45
00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:57.953
Yet they still need Comm to cross.

46
00:02:58.913 --> 00:03:01.473
So Comm has at least one more way to hold Robo back.

47
00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:04.127
What that is remains debated.

48
00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:07.497
For prediction, you need only the result.

49
00:03:08.377 --> 00:03:13.097
While Comm is present, Robo1 stays low, and the axon hears Slit only weakly.
